[White "Intro: Why 1. d4 + 2. c4 — White Repertoire Roadmap"]
[Black ""]
[Result "*"]

{ Welcome to the Foundations chapter of our White repertoire — mark every entry in this chapter as Informational on Chessable.<br><br>Our goal is a coherent, classical 1. d4 repertoire built to outplay club and tournament opposition from 1800 to 2200+ FIDE while training two core skills: planning with a space advantage and sharp calculation.<br><br>Click through the moves below to preview all eight chapters, our five core structures, and our strict move-order rules. } 1. d4 { We open 1. d4 to claim central space and steer into rich middlegames rather than sterile forced draws.<br><br>Against the Dutch Defense — 1... f5 — we switch immediately into an aggressive London setup with Bf4 and e3 before Nf3, keeping the d1-h5 diagonal open for h4-h5 or g4 attacks. } { [%csl Gd4] [%cal Gc2c4,Bc1f4] } 1... f5 ( 1... d5 { When Black answers 1... d5, 2. c4 challenges the center immediately. Here Black faces major Indian setups as well as 2... e6, the Slav, and the Queen's Gambit Accepted. } { [%cal Gc2c4,Rd5c4] } 2. c4 { Against 2. c4, Black's solid alternatives to 2... e6 are the Slav and the QGA. Remember our structural versatility rule: we reserve main-line fianchetto setups for the Slav and the Tarrasch. } { [%cal Gc4d5,Rd5c4] } 2... c6 ( 2... dxc4 3. e4 { — Chapter 7 QGA: central 3. e4 grabs full space and invites sharp open play.<br><br>Now let us lock in our QGD move order: } ) ( 2... e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 { Move-Order Rule Number 1: always play 4. Bg5 before Nf3! Playing 4. Nf3 allows the Vienna, Classical Ragozin, and Botvinnik complexes.<br><br>Even after 4. Bg5, if Black tries 4... c6, our mandatory safety brake is 5. e3! — never 5. Nf3?. } { [%csl Yg5] [%cal Bg5e7,Bg5h4] } 4... c6 ( 4... Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 { We finish Chapter 1's Classical QGD trunk with 6. Nf3. Every chapter in this course follows the same lean standard: roughly 11 moves average depth, clickable inline sidelines, and a clear Plan From Here summary at every leaf.<br><br>On Chessable import, always leave the sub-variation splitting checkbox UNTICKED so sideline links stay embedded inside each line. } { [%csl Ge3,Gf3] [%cal Bg5h4,Bf1d3,Ra1c1] } ) 5. e3 { — Anti-Botvinnik guard: 5. e3! keeps the diagonal closed and steers cleanly into Orthodox or Cambridge Springs.<br><br>Back to 4... Be7: } ) 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Qc2 { — Chapter 3 Slav Complex: 4. Qc2 followed by g3 is one of only two main-line fianchetto setups alongside the Tarrasch.<br><br>And if Black accepts the gambit?: } ) ( 1... Nf6 2. c4 g6 ( 2... c5 3. d5 e6 4. Nc3 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nf3 g6 7. Bf4 { — Chapter 6 Modern Benoni: concrete 7. Bf4 pressure on d6, meeting 7... Bg7 with 8. Qa4+ and 7... a6 with 8. a4.<br><br>Back to the Nimzo-Indian: } ) ( 2... e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 { — Chapter 2 Nimzo-Indian: the classical Rubinstein 4. e3, 5. Bd3, 6. Nf3 framework.<br><br>Returning to 1... d5 2. c4: } ) 3. Nc3 Bg7 ( 3... d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. e4 Nxc3 6. bxc3 Bg7 7. Nf3 c5 8. Bb5+ { — Chapter 5 Grunfeld Exchange: our signature 8. Bb5+ check disrupts Black's coordination before we consolidate.<br><br>And against 2... c5 or 2... e6 3. Nc3 Bb4?: } ) 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. d5 { — Chapter 4 King's Indian Defense: we choose the principled Petrosian 7. d5, locking the center cleanly and keeping lively ideas like 8. Bg5 h6 9. Be3!.<br><br>What if Black prefers the Grunfeld or Benoni?: } ) 2. Bf4 Nf6 3. e3 { — Chapter 8 Dutch setup: always play e3 before Nf3 so Qf3-g3 and g4 remain deadly.<br><br>Now back to 1... d5 and 2. c4: } *

[Event "Ch0: Foundations — White 1. d4 Repertoire"]
