[White "Intro: Structure 1 — The Carlsbad: Minority Attack vs Central Steamroller"]
[Black ""]
[Result "*"]

{ Structure 1 of 5 — Informational Blueprint.<br><br>Whenever White plays cxd5 and Black recaptures exd5, the Carlsbad pawn structure appears. White's queenside pawn tension turns into two crystal-clear long-term plans depending on where the pieces stand. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 Nbd7 7. Rc1 a6 8. cxd5 { The defining exchange cxd5 freezes the center once Black recaptures on d5, leaving White with a three-versus-two queenside majority against Black's c6 and b7 pawns.<br><br>White trades central tension for an unassailable positional plan: either push b2-b4-b5 to create a backward c6 pawn, or prepare f2-f3 and e3-e4 in the center. } { [%csl Gd5,Rc6] [%cal Gd5e6,Re6d5] } 8... exd5 9. Bd3 c6 10. O-O Re8 11. Qc2 { From this textbook Carlsbad setup, recognize both blueprints at a glance:<br><br>• Plan A — Minority Attack: advance a3, Rb1, and b4-b5 to fracture c6 and leave Black nursing a permanent queenside target.<br><br>• Plan B — Central Steamroller: when Black commits pieces passively or White puts the knight on e2, build f2-f3 and e3-e4 to seize the entire center. } { [%csl Rc6,Ye4] [%cal Ga2a3,Gb2b4,Gf2f3,Ge3e4,Bc1b1] } 11... Nf8 12. Rb1 a5 13. a3 { — Plan A in motion: preparing b2-b4-b5 with rook support. Compare with Plan B when White develops Nge2 in Ch1 Line 5.1. } *

[Event "Ch0: Foundations — White 1. d4 Repertoire"]
